  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_31f9454679c14af89e8f1ef34b0eb725",
      "statement": "Maximum diversification treats diversification as the explicit objective of portfolio construction rather than a side effect of utility optimization; the concept and the diversification ratio were introduced by Choueifaty (2006) and popularized by Choueifaty and Coignard (2008), needing no expected-return estimates and avoiding bets founded on return forecasts or on cap-weighted benchmarks.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L14-L18",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c537ef3259189572c7ef30b9644959fdd880133b843bedaef81a76fd1f9d7583:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7fc777b2eb174fa9b78356c0abc67746",
      "statement": "The diversification ratio of a portfolio is D(w) = <w|sigma>/sigma(w) = w'sigma / sqrt(w'Vw), the weighted average of asset volatilities divided by portfolio volatility.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L22-L28",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1d42895e2ad048a42e6e3e5a6395f5b6c5d2c59941f8a56ff491565dfe14fd0e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dde38becc14c45fcb3452115bf1740bb",
      "statement": "For a long-only portfolio the diversification ratio is always >=1, equals exactly 1 for a mono-asset portfolio, and is strictly greater than 1 unless the portfolio is equivalent to a mono-asset one; N independent assets of equal volatility give D=sqrt(N).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L28-L30",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ab0d803fac607ed86bf5410cc456ba2e86b8c553065cdac8c32151b177692b8e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5ccd6a1d40534b7881e8f1579d92ed32",
      "statement": "For two assets of volatility 15% and 30% with correlation below 1 the diversification-maximizing weights are inversely proportional to volatilities (66.6% on A, 33.3% on B); for two strongly correlated banks (rho=0.9) and one weakly correlated pharma (rho=0.1) of equal volatility the weights are 25.7% each bank and 48.6% pharma.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L32-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0d730dc9f3c8c69356e3cf482c90e9ae5c07d2663491ace9a9f7debfa1ebb9c0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e7fd49f7fcb843d9b84e4e88fc94dc90",
      "statement": "The diversification ratio decomposes as 1/D(w)^2 = (1-rho(w)) CR(w) + rho(w), where rho(w) is the volatility-weighted average correlation and CR(w) = sum_i(w_i sigma_i)^2 / (sum_i w_i sigma_i)^2 is the volatility-weighted concentration ratio; a mono-asset portfolio has CR=1 and a volatility-equal-weighted portfolio the minimum CR=1/N.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L40-L52",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8a213fe2d9d369a16023e30f1d92f92b4abd885dab31851683904dbefd27c32b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_007b360d073646be803982814857fe9b",
      "statement": "D grows as average correlation and/or the concentration ratio fall; if correlations tend to 1 then D=1 regardless of CR, and when pairwise correlations are equal maximizing D reduces to minimizing CR. D^2 reads as the number of independent risk factors (degrees of freedom): MSCI World had D=1.7 at end-2010 (about 3 factors), and maximizing would reach D=2.6 (about 7).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L54-L56",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d75d18db5405d5f7578d9ae743825a5c47434c8e90c2a2338e619a1d2d166871:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a3815ecab1504e6e914206a58fbfe889",
      "statement": "Because the diversification ratio is invariant to scalar rescaling of the weights, maximizing it is equivalent to the quadratic program min (1/2) w'Vw subject to w_i>=0 and sum_i w_i sigma_i = 1 (with weights rescaled to sum to 1 afterward); this QP over a convex set has a solution, unique if V is definite.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L60-L70",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b6e4f9e44b91b1b7a2a0a3cc3f5c4b122a8ec0dc997c56c610bfdd850a6c0281:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7ce3566732b74ddbaa475cf507ba21d8",
      "statement": "Applying the KKT theorem to f(w)=ln D(w), the Most-Diversified Portfolio optimum satisfies V w^MDP = (sigma(w^MDP)/D(w^MDP)) sigma + lambda with min(lambda, w^MDP)=0 and non-negative duals, and the stationarity condition is independent of the sum-to-one constraint.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L72-L84",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a159462bf064fd634f50548b4021cbc4cd1b009a0c87e81ab0a00c599d85694e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c216b173b49a4b369a26cae4b8d9f722",
      "statement": "The Most-Diversified Portfolio is the minimum-variance portfolio computed on the correlation matrix: defining synthetic unit-volatility assets Y_i = X_i/sigma_i + (1-1/sigma_i)$ makes the synthetic covariance matrix equal the original correlation matrix C, so maximizing the synthetic diversification ratio is minimizing S'CS.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L86-L98",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cd7cdbe656183e868fb1baa5d26189ec9c746d50287ce3bf5a8ec0f61645c431:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ff4678e2264b4965a9035c94b69ec62a",
      "statement": "When C is invertible and unconstrained the synthetic solution is S proportional to C^{-1} 1, and reconstructing the real assets by dividing each synthetic weight by its volatility and rescaling to 100% invested gives M proportional to sigma^{-1} C^{-1} 1 (sigma the diagonal volatility matrix).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L100-L108",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ab0fdba5ac4b38a617195b9410b4d8da3c019c7d9b3201c26020b29e8e4a76b1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_24026053c8474e0bb065b32f23173765",
      "statement": "Since V M = kappa sigma, the correlation of any portfolio P with the MDP is rho_{P,M} = D(P) kappa/sigma_M, proportional to P's diversification ratio; applied to a single asset (D=1) it gives rho_{i,M} = kappa/sigma_M identical for every asset, so the MDP is the portfolio in which all assets share the same positive correlation with it, and rho_{P,M} = D(P)/D(M).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L110-L130",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fab61c97e3a9f3614b09f3cd26d19496e50c4cfc77ff79c392cb929b6b65405e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e2e83773e93b483ab1cdca4e0da65c80",
      "statement": "The core property (constrained long-only case): every asset not held by the MDP is more correlated with it than any held asset, and all held assets share the same correlation with it; equivalently, for any long-only portfolio w, rho between w and the MDP is at least D(w)/D(w^MDP), so all universe assets are effectively represented even if not physically held (an MDP on a 500-stock index may hold about 50).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L132-L140",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5ce269295f972d93926260b7cff6ec17bd8de8f8202fbe00e5d209a2db6fdb6b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0a8b73999bbb464080ff9f4a66087a39",
      "statement": "The MDP satisfies three invariance properties formalized by Choueifaty, Froidure and Reynier (2013): duplication invariance, leverage invariance, and polico (positive-linear-combination) invariance; in a two-asset comparison the MDP satisfies all three while minimum variance is duplication-invariant only, ERC is leverage-invariant only, and equal weighting satisfies none.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L142-L152",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:88927bb58d5d05fa8eb7f44091a45ccc9526f34600e1380232cab4b9ebb4de2f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_26137558643b4239960d2a536b76105a",
      "statement": "If all assets share the same volatility the Most-Diversified Portfolio coincides with the global minimum-variance portfolio, because minimizing S'CS on the correlation matrix then equals minimizing w'Vw on the covariance matrix up to scale.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L156-L158",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b7aa4af33eb4f280756f7650dc12f6e0119859fb86491dfd7d42f946d4c44afd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_facdbb7f5999437d98e7c981eb21fd1f",
      "statement": "The MDP is mean-variance optimal exactly when assets' excess expected returns are proportional to their volatilities (E(r_i)-r_f = k sigma_i): then E(r_w)-r_f = k sigma(w) D(w), so maximizing the diversification ratio equals maximizing the Sharpe ratio and the MDP is the tangency portfolio, with a security-market-line relation in which the MDP plays the market portfolio's role.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L160-L172",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a4b57c26a5ad239fedb40cb67ee2e395ecf30f125cdef767f61502e0a09e9efb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_69d11cda819a4b2cb725265f0b8e892a",
      "statement": "Empirically, long-only MDPs built monthly on US and Eurozone equity universes (1992-2008) with covariance from 250 daily returns and a 4% per-asset risk-contribution cap earned higher risk-adjusted returns than the cap benchmark, minimum variance and equal weighting at lower volatility than the index (13.9% vs 17.9% Eurozone; 12.7% vs 13.4% US); on MSCI World (1999-2010) the MDP had the highest diversification ratio and Sharpe ratio while minimum variance realized the lowest ex post volatility.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/19_maximum_diversification.md#L174-L180",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7bcdc04bdd3ccf11a14d0c99e1b9fa634bc387a1e5d81a16bbd1aaa7572ed0a6:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
